>  The problem is that different schools have different requirements. At
>Rice, optics is offered as a 200 level Physics course. At other schools,
>it's offered at the 400 level. Say I transfer to a school where it is
>offered only as a 400 level course, and it's required. How does my course
>translate? Should it be considered equivelant to their 400 level course,
>even though it almost certainly isn't? Should it be considered the same as
>whatever they offered as a 200 level course, even though they don't cover
>the same material? (What if one school is on the quarter system, and the
>other uses semesters? How do you translate hours?)
>  Since schools can have such different offerings and requirements, expecting
>to be able to painlessly switch schools strikes me as a trifle unrealistic.

Not really.  That's what standards boards are about, and frankly, if you
want to play standards games with the accrediting bodies then I can
certainly argue that the programs ought to be transferrable.  Particularly
within the specialty (ie: if I am a CS major, the core CS courses should be
in some way standardized)

I could propose something else instead, which universities won't like AT ALL.
How's this strike you?

a)	Universities are held to provide professional services to an
	untrained body of people.  Thus, they have a professional level 
	of duty in disclosure and performance. (see the UCC for a more wordy
	version of this standard of performance)  In fact, I think a case
	could be made that this standard of performance should apply under
	common law today.  Perhaps KC could sue on this basis?

b)	From (a), we come up with a liability formula which basically
	states:
	1)	It is reasonable to expect that full legal due process
		will be followed prior to imposition of any sanction.  That
		is, if you don't prove it in a courtroom, you can't apply
		sanctions.
	2)	Sanctions cannot exceed that applied legally (ie: if you're
		fined by a court, then that's it -- you've paid the penalty.
		Same for time served if appropriate).  Thus, the entire
		university sanction idea goes out the window.  Make an
		exception for torts against the university for which no
		legal remedy exists (ie: academic dishonesty), and spell out
		each act and sanction <explicitly>.  Those must be tried by
		a jury of peers as in a courtroom, and you must get a
		"conviction".  No disciplinary hearings allowed.  If its not
		in the manual as a violation, then it isn't punishable.  
	3)	If the school violates this standard then you are entitled
		to a full refund of all monies paid to date, PLUS monetary
		damages amounting to $X for each month which you spent
		attempting to earn your degree (which you've now been
		denied/delayed).  (Ie: if you're "suspended" for a year in
		violation of this, you're owed $12X).  I would also impose
		punitive damages of $10,000 or one year's tuition and fees
		per occurrance as an incentive to colleges to not do this
		kind of thing.

c)	The standard of care due to students is a professional one (ie: you
	can also sue for professional malpractice or malfeasance)

d)	Your academic record is your property.  Period.

I'm sure that schools won't like <that> idea.  If you're going to prevent
free transfer of credits, however, you need to do something to allow a check
and balance for the student's rights.  As things stand now students have NO
rights when it comes to these matters.  This must change.

When I was in college I was rather severely abused by the ACC staff 
(including having a magtape which I owned erased -- without compensation -- 
by the staff because they "suspected" that it might have something naughty 
on it).  Oh, the operations staff claimed it was an accident -- ignoring
that this was a <magtape> and you had to put a write-enable ring on the 
reel in order to scribble on it!  I should have sued their pants off.
Those involved know who they are; they're on the net.  

That university in question didn't provide me with a single usable skill 
that I did not already have.  The "skills" I gained there have not landed 
me a single job, nor have they helped one iota in that quest.  Their MACRO
instructor at the time was a MATH teacher, and knew less about the 
machine's instruction set than I did after I'd had access to the manuals
for two weeks.  They did, however, take a whole hell of a lot of my money, 
and certainly did abuse due process and nearly every ethical standard in my 
book.  I'd never send my kid there, or to any state school as a result of
their mistreatment.

I could rail on for hours about my distaste for "higher" education in
America.  Particularly when applied to state-run and funded schools.
IMHO they do about as good a job as the Chicago public school system.

I urge anyone who has been abused by a university to seek redress in the
courts, and I urge all incoming students to insist on a contractual model 
for their education, with clear responsibilities on both sides.  The "we'll
take your money and give you a degree if we feel like you're enough of a 
sheep" game has gone on long enough.

