>graduate-student-of-the-semester administrators.

Eric mentions (albeit in passing) a *major* problem that affects
both systems administration and our discussions of academic free-
dom issues.

While many people tend to lump admins into a single group, there
is actually a huge number of 'just keep the darned thing running'
temporary admins.  Of course, these are usually (as Eric mentioned)
graduate students; the high turnover in that particular group tends
to lead to poor administration.  While this is not the optimum en-
vironment, it's completely understandable; heck, I wouldn't have 
time to do this *and* finish a thesis or dissertation!

I've also noticed that most of the "more easygoing" admins in our
discussions are those who are, indeed, full-time admins.  On the
other hand, the "shoot-first-ask-questions-later" folks seem to
be, in most cases, our all-I-worry-about-is-keeping-the-thing-going
counterparts.

When discussing incidents or individual admins in the future, it
would be a good idea to indicate their level of experience.  I've
been an admin for 5 years now; there's a big difference in my per-
spective and that of a 'just keep it running' grad student with a
gazillion other things to do.

--Wes

