If you don't really have the expertise or the time to do a real
administration job, and many administrators don't, it's still possible to 
set your phasers on "Stun" rather than "Annihilate" to make shoot-first-
ask-questions-later a sane, useful policy.  You treat the users with respect,
realize they're your customers and also humans, and escalate problems
to the nearest approriate level, with enough explanation to the
non-technical academics or bureaucrats that they also can react reasonably
rather than cluelessly.  It's ok not to know everything.
If you *know* you don't know enough to explain the technical stuff to them,
you should discuss it with someone who does first; if you *don't* know
enough to know when you don't understand the issues, then you probably
*are* dangerously clueless and shouldn't be doing the job....

I suspect that two reasons that full-time admins are more likely to be
easygoing are that they're more likely to have the technical knowledge,
but they're also more likely to have acquired a lot of it by exploring,
whether by hacking around computers, reading manual pages, or whatever,
and are more likely to be able to tell what's non-malicious exploring,
what's malicious vandalism, and what's non-malicious-but-dangerous-anyway.

I was a full-time admin once, and I'm now on a network that doesn't
have a full-time admin, so I find myself doing a lot of admin-like
stuff anyway.  Some of it's stuff I know; some of it's rebooting the
Macintosh printers for the Department Head who still hasn't caught on
that I'm NOT the local mac expert :-)
