I fear that, on the evidence, there _are_ in fact plenty of people, usually
from the more rabid fundamentalist religious ranks, who would far rather
that their homosexual teenagers suicide; much less embarrasing to them if
you take the long view as many of them profess to. Neither of us are ever
likely to change their "minds".

I can't speak for Canada, but in the ol' USA the proof that kids are not
being taught the 3 Rs is an embedded part of everyday life. First, anyone
who works for a living in any capacity in the general population can easily
cite innumerable examples from their daily contacts; it has become apparent
that a frighteningly large majority of those who've exited high school in
the past twenty years do not possess even minimal competence or fluency in
their supposedly native English, nor in the most fundamental mathematical
skills, yea, even unto things as simple as making change or balancing a
checkbook. Secondly, anyone who has raised kids in that timespan and hence
had even casual contact with our typical public school systems can testify
that an increasing portion of daily class time is taken up with a growing
list of curricular items having zero to do with the 3 Rs - things like
"self-esteem", peer group interaction, diversity and sensitivity
indoctrination, global environmental awareness ... the list goes on, and
it's getting longer every year.

At least on this side of the border, we've increasingly been churning out of
our primary and secondary educational system a horde of illiterates and
innumerates these past couple of decades. No one knows where it will end or
if it can be fixed; neither the educrats who administer the system nor the
teachers have, for the most part, demonstrated anything but resistance to
the notion that a problem even exists, much less that it might be necessary
to institute some fundamental changes in the way we go about educating our
children. I might add that the problems are not entirely the fault of the
schools - the other two legs of the tripod are the parents, and the growth
of television in hours-watched-per-year. 

I wish I had some answers, and I'm glad to have no kids in school.


