I do not display the aforementioned pictures on my computer terminal
in public areas (nor on my private computer, for that matter).
I feel that it is in bad taste to do so.  However I do believe 
that sexually explicit computer material being displayed should 
not be allowed at public sites, as it would be offensive to the 
majority of reasonable people.

I have a problem with the proposal though.

>      Barbara is an undergraduate engineering major. When she goes to
>      the computer labs to do her homework, she finds some of her
>      male peers displaying images of scantily clad females. Barbara
>      feels uncomfortable and out of place in the lab when she sees
>      these pictures. The display of the pictures makes it difficult
>      for her to do her work.

"Scantily clad", in a bathing suit, perhaps?  I feel that a *reasonable*
person would not find another person in a swimsuit as a source of
sexual harassment.  I also feel that a person should not be punished
for displaying such a picture.  Would it be sexual harassment if the
genders were reversed in the above paragraph?                  

I feel that the problem is not one of malicious intent but just poor
etiquette.

Daniel L Moore

