| >David, please define "your" files.
| >
| >Wouldn't those be files that you have access to read?

What?  Are you attributing the ability to access something the same as being allowed
too? Its not the same.

Lets put this in a simple case.

Your father has a closet.  You know your not supposed to be in that closet.  You 
suspect there might be a gun in there, but you know your not allowed in there.  You
go in and find a gun.  You take that gun and try to sneak it into your room.  Now
your father catches you sneaking it out of the closet.

You had access (your father didn't lock the door), but you didn't have the right to
go in there, find the gun and try to leave with it.  In the above case, the father
is partially to blame.  In the same fashion, a UNIX sysadmin who doesn't do 'what
is possible' to protect the system would be at fault.

Now one could say that punishing someone for trying to printout this file is part of the 'security.'  The schools failure, if there is one, is that the person CAN NOT
be punished at the same level of 'breaking into a computer system', at most they
could be punished for 'accessing a system file for unknown intent.'  Going back
to the 'burgular tool' analogy.  You are not charged for 'attempted breaking and
entry' for possesing these tools, your are charged for having the tools, not the
crime itself.

These whole discussion goes back to 'computer access' vs 'actual permission'.  I
belive that having 'access' is not the same as 'actual permission.'   If it was,
a successfull hack would be 'actual permission' and the only wrong thing would
be getting caught.  Now I have heard this rationalization before, and thats all it is a rationalization.  Permission assumes some level of responsibility, here we have none.  We have a person who knew what was in the file; Had to  know what it
signified; and printed out for some unknown intent.  This in itself is abuse of
the computer system.  The fact is concluded when no 'justification' is given.

Now you can go back to the 'innocent until proven guilty', but you run into a
brick wall.  The guilt was proven.  The user DID try to printout a file that
any reasonable (or not so reasonable) knew contained access information that they
knew they shouldn't be looking at.  (Do you have to be told that you should look
through your neighbors garbage to get dirt on them?  Its leagle, but it still isn't
right.  If you do it without a reason, you'll probably be hauled away for
something).  This is guilt.  If the person defended themselves and gave a
justification, then punishment may have been lessened.  The ONLY justifiable reason
would have been 'whoops, thats whats in there?'

Nutshell:

Having access is not the same as authority or permission.  For one reason or
another, that file isn't usually protected, but it doesn't imply that any one
has the authority to print it out.  This printing it out in itself is the 'crime
computer system abuse' and the school should punishes as it will.  My only objection
is that the student should not be treated as if breaking into the system has been
attempted.  Last note.  It is obvious to me that the user is/was either completly
stupid or trying to make a point.  Anyone who has anything resembling brain matter
should realize trying to print out such a file on a system printer that they are
not waiting by is 'up there' with using your own address for the 'dropping off
the ransom' in a kidnapping case (or a Gary Hart maneuver).  Since this isn't a
'criminal case' the school isn't bound by the dictates of a court.  If the dean
feels you were going to do something 'bad' with the info, and you don't defend
the action you will be punished.

Lastly.  Many years ago I ran into a security breach in the MUSIC operating system.
No, I'm not going into details.  By a 'simple mistake', I was able to access
EVERYONEs password and access rights.  I did the proper thing, which was to bring
it to the intention of the sysadmin and through them, to the authors of MUSIC.  If
it was 'I vs them' (which I belive the user involved with the above situation
belives), I may have done something different.  The 'crime' is both ways.  If a
sysadmin allows a 'us vs them' feeling to develop then they are causing their own
potential problems.   Realize it and deal with it.  No matter how good you are,
you are not going to catch all the seacurity problems.  By having the users be your
friends, it may never be an issue.

Chris

