That's clearly true, but there's no need to argue in the abstract
here.  The file in question was the file /etc/passwd, not just any
file.  As such, we can discuss whether that file in particular is
intended for public consumption.  In that light, anyone with any
knowledge of using a UNIX system is aware that the password file can be
examined by anyone, and in fact is always world-readable in order to
make that possible.

>We have a person who knew what was in the file; Had to  know what it
>signified; and printed out for some unknown intent.  This in itself is abuse of
>the computer system.  The fact is concluded when no 'justification' is given.

The password file is read constantly by non-privileged users during the
course of a day.  Sometimes it's done by a utility (like "ls", which
must consult it to print user names in its output), and sometimes it's
done explicitly (like, I want to find accounts with home directories on
a particular disk, or which use a certain shell, or whatever).  That's
what the password file is intended to be used for.

To say that because the file was read for "unknown intent" implies that
users must be prepared to divulge the reasons for each and every action
they take on the machine.  Thus, each time a user invokes "vi" she
better be prepared to tell the admin what she's typing, otherwise
she'll be guilty of using the system reasource with "unknown intent"
and with "no justification".

>The guilt was proven.  The user DID try to printout a file that
>any reasonable (or not so reasonable) knew contained access information 
>that they knew they shouldn't be looking at.

As I explain above, this statement is factually incorrect.  The
password file contains information intended by the designers of the
system to be publically accessible.  Were the file in question the
accidentally-readable mailbox of another user, then the debate would be
entirely different.  However, if some system administrator told me that
I couldn't look at /etc/passwd, I'd find another system or lobby to
have that administrator removed for obvious incompetence.

>Nutshell:
>
>Having access is not the same as authority or permission.  For one reason or
>another, that file isn't usually protected, but it doesn't imply that any one
>has the authority to print it out.

Do your homework.  Find out how UNIX systems work.  The file is never protected,
because if it were dozens of utilities wouldn't work.  It is always intended
to be publically readable.

