[Disclaimer: I was involved in the creation of the policy, so I'm even
less objective that usual.]

No where does the recommendation say that any "sexual" image
constitutes harassment. I thought the recommended policy was clear
that sexual harassment via computer is defined with the same
definition and is handled with the same procedure as other types
sexual harassment.

In the recommended example, there isn't just a sexual image, there is
a sexual image that makes it difficult for a student to work. Like it
or not, that basically fits the definition of sexual harassment.  (I
say "basically" because if her difficulty isn't reasonable, then it
doesn't fit the legal definition.)

So, there are 3 questions:

1) Is the legal definition of sexual harassment among students too broad?
2) Is the general U. of Illinois definition broader than the law allows?
3) Is the recommended computer policy broader that the general university
    definition or the legal definition?

Questions 1 and 2 are interesting, but for the purpose of this policy,
they are just assumed to be answered "no".

Question 3 is the most important question here. If you think the
answer is yes, please post.

