]       "Sexual harassment is any unwanted sexual gesture, physical
]        contact, or statement that a reasonable person would find
]        offensive, humiliating, or an interference with his or her
]        required tasks or career opportunities at the University."

][To be legal, I think "or an interference" should be replaced with
]"and an interference"]

Clearly, the campus policy addresses affronts of a personal nature made
towards the person (allegedly) being harrassed.  The new computing 
services policy goes beyond e-mail harrassment into the realm of expression
at large, and even the viewing images for personal satisfaction only--without
intention of attracting attention. 

There are already means of dealing with waste/abuse of computing resourses.
There is already a clear and somewhat reasonable policy for dealing with
sexual harrassment at UIUC.  The new policy goes beyond either, and 
elevates both tastelessness and innocent viewing of non-obscene images to
punishable offenses. 

A policy which would be much less threatening to academic freedom, waste a
lot less paper, cause less confusion as to interpretation, and not SEEM so
draconian (even if our new one really isn't) follows: 

"Viewing picture files and listening to sound files, except for classwork
or other acedemic endeavors, is gaming.  As games, these actions 
are restricted to hours when game playing is allowed.  Gaming which 
interferes with (per the UIUC harrassment or CCSO policies) a person's work,
even at another terminal, because they can see the image or hear the sound
and find it offensive, is not allowed.  People in violation will be asked
to shut off the game or to move to another terminal.  Offenders who don't
comply with this request will be ejected from the lab for violation of 
gaming rules.  Turning off the image/sound is compliance, and no report
will be made.  Offenders repeatedly ejected from a lab for violation of
gaming rules will be subject to disciplinary or administrative action
consistent with CCSO rules.  

"An explicit, but non-obscene background image, displayed while other work
is being performed, is passive political expression, and is not prohibited.
Taste and good manners are encouraged, but not required, in selecting 
a background image.  If informed that your background image is offensive,
you are strongly encouraged, but in no way required, to shut it off in the
interests of a comfortable learning environment for all."

This is my idea of a compromise.  In actuality, no expression, electronic
or otherwise, not directed at another person with the intention of causing
discomfort or offense, should ever be prohibited under any circumstances.
Speaking at large should never be an offense, provided such speech is not 
illegal. 

