about sexual harrassment in the workplace or in college.  :-) :-) :-)
Ok, before anyone starts tossing their terminals at me, please read
the rest of this posting before getting too angry. :-)

It seems that if women want to place pictures of scantily clad men in
the workplace, they can get away with it.  However, if a man were to
place a picture of a scantily clad women on the wall next to his desk,
he'd be accused of harassment.  This is how it was at one company
where I worked.  However, I fail to see how displaying semi-nude
pictures on computer screens can constitute harassment; no one is
being forced to look at them.  How does this differ from when one goes
to the beach and sees many scantily clad people?  Oversensitivity
should not be confused with harassment and society should not cater to
overly sensitive people.

Now, I will agree that many forms of harassment are displays of poor
manners and no gentleman (or no lady, in a reverse situation) would
perform such harassment.  Unfortunately, morals have sunk so low that
a lot of things go on in the workplace that shouldn't.  Yes, it is
offensive if a man walks up to a woman at work and says eomething like
"whoa! look at that pair of hooters!", and then reaches for them.
Likewise, it is just as offensive to hear a group of women talking
about some man's "buns".  Both are examples of behavior that have no
place in the workplace, etc., and people who exhibit this sort of
behavior, in public, need to be frowned upon by their peers.

However, some people take the issue of harassment to extremes, such as
innocent flirtation.  Men will, naturally, flirt with women.
Likewise, women will, naturally, flirt with men.  Men will often leer
at women who are attractive and who dress provocatively.  If women
don't want to be stated at, they they shouldn't wear slit skirts or
tight leather skirts, dieplay a lot of cleavage, etc.  However, when
this sort of bahavior is defined as harassment, as some people say
that it is, then society also has a problem.

Perhaps we need to just go back to relying more upon teaching people
good manners instead of making up a lot of rules to deal with
harassment.  It seems like a much more sensible solution.


