The "appeal" meeting was held at 3pm Thursday (yesterday).
It was tape-recorded, so possibly it will be transcribed in the near future.

I had the thought that it would be an informal discussion type, but there was
an agenda which was strictly followed (directed by Dean Munson).

The agenda went something like:  Opening statement (10 minutes each),
Rebuttal (10 min. each), and Closing statements (5 min. each).

In each of the three parts, I went first and Support Services went after.
I feel this is to their advantage, but this didn't really hit me till the
Closing statement when Bill Schubert (of SS) closed with something like 
"James is the only student I know who has violated policy three times.  I know
 of only two other students who have violated policy twice," and I wanted to
respond to this, but could not. 

Avi & I had hoped that they would bring up the past first, and they did,
but in the end when we couldn't respond.  First of all, I have not violated
policy three times...this "unauthorized use/access" is still pending a
decision from Dean Munson; and I don't believe me being found in violation
of the "recreational use" policy by using IRC is fair at all, as this was not
even considered a violation in Rowley's referral (case2).  So, I'm not a repeat
trouble-maker.  I do not wish to intentionally break the rules, but I do wish
to use my rights and privileges as much, and as far, as I can and am able to.
And second, what does my previous violations have to do with whether or not
I am guilty or not-guilty of the current case?  This just enforced my opening
statement which said, "I feel I have been labeled a 'trouble maker' as a 
result of trying to educate myself in these specialized areas [referring to
unix security and client-server games...this is part of my past, but I did
not go into anything about my previous violations related to these.] ...
I feel a certain prejudice because of this, and it seems I have been singled
out and certain policies are being selectively enforced on me."

Bill Schubert and Tom Monroe were the present Support Services people.
Their stance was basically that the account is for instructional use, and I
was given immediate permission (for 2 hours) to backup my account.  Tom
Monroe says I was allowed to get *my* files only, and I violated this
permissions/authorization by accessing a file which is not owned by me --
that is, it is owned by root (Asst. Manager of MENUDO) and I am not allowed
to access other users' files.

I mentioned the /etc/motd file.  This is owned by root, no?

Avi made a good point that there is no clear, written policy on "immediate
access" given to a student.  I pointed out that I felt I had general access
for 2 hours...but, printing out the /etc/passwd file, or accessing it through
cat or grep, is considered a violation by UH SS no matter what, since it is
only accessable by root, in their terms.

So, moreover, they say you can only use instructional accounts for
instructional purposes, and that you can't access -- in any way -- files not
owned by you.  What if another user mailed /etc/passwd to me?  It'd be mine.
What if I had copied it two weeks earlier?  It'd be mine (have my uid).
But, supposedly NOBODY can access it in ANYWAY, except the user root.
And then, there is no written policies for instructional accounts.  This type
of account falls under the "Usenet, email, editting" purposes...and I claimed
that the /etc/passwd is for email purposes.

This shows, I believe, that either their egos have been threatened because
they think I went against Monroe's "authorization" intentionally, or that
they are saying this to cover up their mistake in the ORIGINAL assumption that
my action was a security concern.  Indeed, the kid picking print-outs noticed
it was the "/etc/passwd" so he wasn't thinking, "Oh, non-class related
output...I think I'll report this to SS."  He was thinking "hacker!"
And in Monroe's memo to Schubert, the subject is "Security report..."

Well, hopefully Dean Munson will realize how ridiculous, petty, and small 
this all is (though it's been blown-up by the public, which is only the right
response since most people agree this is ridiculous, but I feel it may have
too much attention), and I hope he investigates the print-outs made by other
class accounts, because he will indeed discover non-class related output
which might also be a file not owned by that user (/etc/motd for instance)!
And that there is no difference between /etc/motd and /etc/passwd in regards
to security.

And I hope he realizes that the charges were vague, and re-worded, and I was
not prepared to adequately defend myself (in the original conference with
Anzivino)...meaning, you CAN NOT change the charge and think up possible
violations at the last minute because it so happens to fall under the Handbook
policy 3.14 ("Unauthorized use or misuse of computers...") which isn't too
hard since this is extremely broad and everything falls under it!

I suggest, in the future, that the Dean of Student point out either the
Computing Facilities Usage Guideline, or Security Guideline for Users, which
is in question.

In SS's stance, the charge was changed to #1 ("To respect the privacy of
other users; for example, users shall not intentionally...obtain copies of...
files belonging to other users...") and #3 ("To respect the intended usage
for which access to computing resources was granted; for example...the
production of output that is unrelated to the objectives of the project...").

They could have just told me "Oh, it's number one and three," but NO...
...I had to play the guessing game.


