| Well, the obvious question is: why is printing /etc/passwd not
| allowed?  Its permissions are -rw-r--r--, the same as about 14,000
| other files in another directory (/local/src - which contains such
| radical, obviously off-limits information such as source code for
| xmodem, sz, xv, archie, gopher, wais, elm, to say nothing of the gnu
| subdirectory with bison, flex, perl, emacs, etc.)  Heaven forbid if I
| have the sudden desire to study regexp.c (1223 lines, which is longer
| than /etc/passwd at 960 lines).
|

The 'why' is that it is a file that is obviously for system purposes and by its
name may have secure information in it.  The etc/ directory commonly has files
that are not for user use, this is one of them.

Studying the files in /local/src has value and is something that no one would object
to.

Just because a files permissions are such that you CAN access it, doesn't mean you
MAY access it.  Even the most UNIX novice people I talked too about this issue (i.e.
just finished first year students), said that they user should not have accessed the
file, even with the appropriate access settings.

As I said in my earlier post,  since this is a 'open for read' file, if a user
claims ignorance of the purposes of the file, then this would be a potential excuse
(depending on the school policies).  Several schools specifically tell students
NOT to access any system files.

In any case, the accessing of ANY file on a school (or company) system can require a
justification.  There are valid reasons why a user may want access to this file.  If
those reasons were explained, then it may be excused (depending on school policy).
By not explaining, and not claiming ignorance, the student invites the school to think of worse case situations and deal with them accordingly.

Wrap up
-------

On computer systems access <> authority.  The idea is that access SHOULD = authority
to make it clear for everyone.  Since this isn't a perfect world however, many of
us get access to things that call for us to make a judgment call.  This is the realm
of ethics.  By accessing a file by the name passwrd, in a sub-directory of 'system
data files' should give one pause.  If there is still a justifiable reason for the
access that can be explained, then the judgement call can be made to print it out.
The school officials, however, MAY ask what that reason was.

This works for computer systems and any 'system.'  Just because you can, doesn't
say you may.  Think of your childhood, and you'll see many examples.  It is the
hackers psychology that claims the opposite, that access ALWAYS = authority.  If you
can get at something, your allowed too.

If you want to make the argument that by setting such permissions, the system
administration has given AUTHORITY to all users, thats fine.  The school, however,
can still ask the user for the justification.  This is their system, and the rules
are theirs.

